  {
    "original": "Welcome & communication",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "The handbook is a reference for orchestra events, responsibilities, and behavior. Alex Figueroa welcomes new and returning musicians to the McKay orchestra program.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Questions and concerns should be sent to director Alex Figueroa at figueroa_alexander@salkeiz.k12.or.us. Email is the preferred way to get in touch.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Instruments & rental support",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "The handbook recommends Uptown Music and Willamette Valley Music equally for instrument rentals and supplies. Their listed contact details appear below.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "If renting an instrument is not possible, contact the director to work out a plan. The same applies if acquiring required equipment is difficult.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Although the original table of contents lists school-owned instruments, the supplied edition does not contain a separate policy section. Ask the director about current availability.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Uptown Music · 3827 River Rd N, Keizer, OR 97303 · 503-393-4437",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Willamette Valley Music · 484 State St, Salem, OR 97301 · 503-385-8790",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Purchasing an instrument",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Consult the orchestra director before purchasing an instrument. Compare new and used options from several dealers, set a budget, and arrange appointments to try instruments in person.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Bring the student and their music. Narrow the choice to two or three instruments, then ask the director for guidance. The handbook discourages buying from unreliable online sellers without testing the instrument.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Equipment checklist",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Students should have the following supplies. Speak with the director if you need help obtaining them.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Cleaning cloth",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Rock stop for cello and bass",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Reliable shoulder rest for violin and viola",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Music stand",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Instrument care & repair",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Use instrument polish from a music dealer. Household polish can damage the varnish and should not be used.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Do not attempt repairs yourself. Bring a damaged instrument to school first so the director can assess it. If it cannot be repaired with school materials, take it to a music store.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Concert uniforms",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "The 2021–2022 handbook states that the school provides all-black orchestra uniforms: a black dress shirt with black pants, or a black dress.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Students supply their own long black socks and black shoes. Confirm current uniform arrangements with the director.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Concert attendance & absences",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "In the archived policy, attendance at every participating ensemble performance is mandatory. Concert dates were shared at the beginning of the year so families could plan around them.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "For a planned absence, a parent or guardian had to provide a written note with the performance date, reason, and daytime phone number by October 1, 2021. An excused-absence assignment was due the day before the concert or before leaving for the planned destination.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Illness and a death in the family were listed as emergency excused absences. A note was required on the first day back at school.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Missing the advance-note deadline or failing to provide an emergency note could result in an unexcused absence. The policy listed avoidable conflicts such as work, family birthdays, and transportation problems. The director encouraged families to communicate when problems arose.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "The historical consequence was a reduction of two letter grades and an additional assignment due one week after the concert. Not submitting it resulted in one further letter-grade reduction. These are archived rules; request current policies before relying on them.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Concert behavior",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "The archived handbook sets these performance expectations:",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Arrive at the assigned call time, allowing time to prepare.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Stay for the full concert to support the other ensembles unless excused by the director with a parent or guardian note.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Leaving without an approved excuse reduced the concert grade by 50%, described as a one-letter drop in the orchestra grade.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Remain quiet and respectful while other ensembles perform.",
    "group": "Handbook"
  },
  {
    "original": "Follow the McKay Student Code of Conduct.",
